ce:section.ce:para:
Four CSTRs were used in this work: three pilot reactors (PR), two with working capacities of 100 L and one with 80 L; and one laboratory reactor (R65) with a 20 L working capacity (Table 2
                         and Fig. 1
                        ). The three PRs used are of similar design, using water-filled heating tubes inside the reactor to control the temperature. The body of the reactor carries an insulating jacket to ensure a stable operating temperature. In the R65 reactor, the temperature is controlled by electrical resistances located at the bottom of the reactor. In all reactors, continuous mixing was performed by a central shaft with a propeller at the bottom rotating at 140 rpm.

The control reactor (PR1) showed stable performances in terms of solids concentration, solids removal and pH during the experimental period (Tables 3 and 4

                        ). Small differences between periods 2 and 3 were found in the average TAN concentration and SO4
                        2−, these parameters being higher in period 3 than in period 2 (Tables 3 and 4, respectively). However, the SO4
                        2− reduced from the influent stream (Table 4), and the H2S concentration in the biogas (Fig. 2
                        ) were very low in both periods (<10% and <3500 ppm, respectively).
